meta {
  name: List Users
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users?pageSize=100
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  pageSize: 100
  ~page: 1
  ~q: producer
  ~status: active,invited
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

script:post-response {
  // Capture an active, non-owner user for the removal requests that follow.
  // Picked by role rather than hard-coded id so the collection works against
  // any seeded tenant.
  //
  // ⚠ `.items`, not the body — this endpoint returns a paginated envelope
  // since PAC-101, and `pageSize=100` above is what keeps the producer on the
  // first (only) page rather than trusting the default of 25. Get this wrong
  // and `activeUserId` stays unset, which breaks `Send Password Reset`,
  // `Deactivate User` and `Reactivate User` with a request to `/users//…`.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const target = res.getBody().items.find(
      (u) => u.isActive && !u.deactivatedAt && u.email === bru.getEnvVar("producerEmail"),
    );
    if (target) bru.setVar("activeUserId", target._id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a paginated envelope, not a bare array", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("page");
    expect(body).to.have.property("pageSize");
    expect(body).to.have.property("total");
    expect(body).to.have.property("totalPages");
    expect(body.items).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("ranks invited before active before deactivated", function () {
    const rank = function (u) {
      return u.deactivatedAt ? 2 : u.isActive ? 1 : 0;
    };
    const ranks = res.getBody().items.map(rank);
    const sorted = ranks.slice().sort();
    expect(ranks).to.deep.equal(sorted);
  });

  test("every row carries the three-way status fields", function () {
    const user = res.getBody().items[0];
    expect(user).to.have.property("isActive");
    // `isActive` alone is ambiguous — it is false for both a pending invite and
    // a removed employee. `deactivatedAt` is what separates them.
    expect(user).to.have.property("deactivatedAt");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /users — the agency directory (paginated since PAC-101)

  Every user in the caller's agency, one page at a time. Platform admins are
  excluded — they are not the agency's to manage.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:read`.

  ⚠ **This returned a bare array until PAC-101.** It is now the paginated
  envelope every other list uses. A caller that wants *everybody* — a picker —
  wants **`GET /users/options`** instead; that is where the three `<Select>`
  components that used to read this endpoint went, and why this shape could
  change with one consumer to update.

  ## Query
  | param | type | default | notes |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | page | int | 1 | 1-based |
  | pageSize | int | 25 | max 100 |
  | q | string | — | max 120; tokenized — see below |
  | status | multi | — | `active` \| `invited` \| `deactivated`, ORed |

  ### How `q` is interpreted

  Lower-cased, accent-stripped, split into at most 4 tokens; every token must
  match something, but not all in the same field. Each is a case-insensitive
  substring against `firstName`, `lastName`, `email`, the user's **role names**,
  and — for a caller who holds `agency:branches:read` — their **branch name**.

  ⚠ **The branch arm is permission-gated.** The Branch column only renders for a
  caller with `agency:branches:read`, so ORing it in unconditionally would
  return rows matching a column they cannot see: a search that reads as broken,
  and a branch-name oracle. Every *default* role holding `agency:users:read`
  also holds `agency:branches:read`, so this only bites a custom role — which is
  exactly what it is defending.

  `status` is a **facet, not searchable text**: it is derived from `isActive` +
  `deactivatedAt` rather than stored, so matching it as text would need an
  `$expr` and would make `act` hit both "active" and "deactivated".

  ## Sort
  `deactivatedAt, isActive, lastName, firstName, email` under the `en`/strength-2
  collation. BSON orders **Null before Date**, so that reproduces the ranking
  the page used to do in the browser — invited, then active, then deactivated —
  without a computed stage. `email` is the tiebreak, so paging is deterministic.

  **Response `200`** — `{ page, pageSize, total, totalPages, items }`. Secrets
  (`passwordHash`, `inviteToken`, `passwordResetToken`) are never returned.

  ## Reading the status — do not branch on `isActive` alone
  `isActive: false` covers **two different states**:

  | State       | `isActive` | `deactivatedAt` |
  |-------------|------------|-----------------|
  | Active      | `true`     | `null`          |
  | Invited     | `false`    | `null`          |
  | Deactivated | `false`    | set             |

  A client that checks only `isActive` will render a removed employee as
  "Invited" and offer to resend them an invite. The web app derives this once in
  `userStatus()` (`packages/web/src/lib/users-api.ts`) — do the same.

  **Errors**
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:read`.
}
